    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d164">

                <pb facs="0404" n="382" sameAs="frus:frus1950v05/pg382"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">
                    S/P–NSC Fites:
                    Lot 61D167: Greece, U.S. Position with Respect to NSC 103, 103/1<anchor n="1" xml:id="d164-f1"/>
                </note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State for Near
                        Eastern, South Asian and African Affairs</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">McGhee</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Deputy under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Matthews</hi>)<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d164-f2"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1950-07-22">July 22, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Problem: To determine United States action in the event of an
                    attack against Greece by Soviet satellite forces.</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Discussion</hi>
                </p>
                <p>Attached hereto is a paper setting forth our analysis of a possibility of attack
                    by Soviet satellite forces against Greece. It is our conclusion that, in the
                    event of an attack against Greece, the United States should initiate and support
                    United Nations police action to the maximum extent consistent with the overall
                    demands against United States military resources.</p>
                <pb facs="0405" n="383" xml:id="pg383"/>
                <p rendition="#flush">
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Recommendation</hi>
                </p>
                <p>That this paper be processed for ultimate consideration by the National Security
                    Council.</p>


                <xi:include href="d164-a1.xml" xpointer="d164-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d164-f1" n="1" xml:id="d164-f1-content">
                <p>Lot 61 D 167 is a serial file of memoranda relating to National Security Council
                    questions for the years 1950–1961, as maintained by the Policy Planning Staff of
                    the Department of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d164-f2" n="2" type="source" xml:id="d164-f2-content">
                <p>H. Freeman Matthews. On this paper there appears a note which states that it was
                    sent to the NSC staff on August 3, 1950.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
